INSERT INTO `tmp_en_page_text_Text` VALUES
('4394', 'The land here is harsh. Beyond the Dojan Ruins lie the Krasarang Wilds. It feels like my fur will never dry.$b$bWorse still, a boundless sorrow seems to hang over the whole region. I''m no shaman or druid, but even I can tell when the Light has abandoned a place.$b$bMounted patrols of lizard-folk appear from time to time. The locals call them the "sorenn," and every last one of them has a foul temper.$b$bTo top it all off, I met a group of priests who had fled the Temple of the Red Crane after the sorrow took form and began to spread, attacking everything in its path.$b$bThese poor souls must be protected, and I will do everything in my power to see it done.'),
('4455', 'The hozen tongue remains a great mystery. Nearly all hozen know the common tongue, yet they prefer to pepper their speech with many sounds and "words" that have so far defied translation.$b$bJinyu scholars suspect these expressions are most likely insults, but nothing is certain yet.$b$bAs the great priest Uk-Uka said, "Every derka has its jiba, but not every jiba has its derka."$b$bTruly wise words. Probably.'),
('4499', 'The saurok were meant to be bound warriors in the service of the mogu empire, a fierce and frenzied people. After the Culling, they lay hidden for many centuries in the Krasarang Jungle and in the swamps beyond the Serpent''s Spine.$b$bOf late, the saurok have grown bold and begun raiding the coastal cities of Pandaria, vanishing into the sea with their plunder.$b$bIf you meet a saurok in the jungle, know that its kin are surely nearby. Be ready to defend yourself!'),
('4500', 'The saurok were originally created to keep order on the far borders of the mogu empire. Saurok culture was founded on a sense of their own superiority and hatred of any weakness, a mindset the conduct of their masters did much to encourage.$B$BSoon the saurok guardians began to plunder those they served. They stopped obeying orders and attacked their own commanders whenever they felt they had been given too little loot.$B$BEmperor Dojan answered as the mogu do: the souls were torn from the bodies of all the saurok in the Vale of Eternal Blossoms. He ordered the same punishment for the legions in the field. Thus began the great Culling.'),
('4507', 'Unlike the pandaren and the mogu, who work with fire and forge, the Jinyu have mastered the secrets of water. They make metal and stone change their shape using the pressure of water and the force of the current.$b$bAt first the Jinyu simply gathered water-polished stones and other materials from the rivers. They learned to find the places where the finest stones could be found.$b$bOver hundreds of years, the Jinyu learned to command water with magic and to build astonishing structures of stone and other materials that have no equal. Their weapons and armor are as strong as tempered steel, and their walls seem to be built without a single seam.'),
('4516', 'Every hundred years, newborn mantid hatch from their eggs in vast numbers. In time the horde inevitably migrates east, devouring everything in its path until it reaches the Serpent''s Spine. There they die by the thousands at the hands of the pandaren, just as they once fell to the mogu who raised that wall.$b$bPandaren scholars have tried to study this cycle, but have never reached agreement. Why do the mantid let their young perish again and again? Only the mantid themselves know for certain.$b$bThe cycle has become so predictable that the guardians of the wall prepare for it well in advance every century. The Shado-Pan, like the mogu before them, know when the horde will come and plan their defense accordingly. As long as the wall stands, Pandaria has a shield against the seemingly endless mantid horde.'),
('4517', 'The mantid assault on the Serpent''s Spine is a true horror, for defenders and attackers alike. Only the cleverest, strongest, and craftiest mantid can survive the slaughter, and the pandaren losses are simply beyond counting.$b$bThe surviving mantid return to the shade of the great trees with their battle trophies. They return home and take their place in mantid society according to their deeds.$b$bThe purpose of these ritual raids is unclear, but those who travel beyond the wall remember the warning: any mantid met out there is a battle-hardened soldier, to be feared and respected.'),
('4519', 'The Mantid Empress is a power that all of Pandaria fears. It is she who spawns the countless mantid hordes. $b$bEmpresses live long, but they are not eternal. The Klaxxi, the council of mantid elders, oversee the passing of power to a new empress. The details of this succession are not known for certain, but it includes trials of combat. The new empress devours the remains of her predecessor.  In this way, the succession has remained unbroken throughout the history of mantid civilization.$b$bThe Klaxxi, though few in number, undoubtedly play a key role in creating and preserving mantid culture. Nevertheless, it is unclear whether they can directly oppose the will of the empress.'),
('4524', '"In the days of the mogu, slaves were the lifeblood of the empire. Pandaren, hozen, and Jinyu labored in the fields and the mines, and built mighty strongholds at the will of their masters.$b$bSo that weariness and injury would not hinder the work, the mogu chose certain pandaren slaves and ordered them to brew healing potions. At first these were merely herbal infusions and poultices. From these humble healers came the healers, leaders, and brewmasters of later days.$b$bThus a noble tradition was born, and the first ''monks'' became the hope of the pandaren, the embodiment of their pride.$b$bIt was they who once founded the art of fighting without weapons and secretly taught it to other slaves. During the uprising, the monks were the first into battle, inspiring the farmers, smiths, and masons..."'),
('4532', 'Burning oil is the yaungol''s chief weapon. In the days when their greatest warriors walked the world, it outstripped magic in deadliness. The oil crippled enemies, scorched the earth, destroyed trees and the cover they provided, and put even mantid armies to flight.$b$bThe oldest yaungol tactic is to light far more fires at night than the warriors need. An enemy watching the yaungol after sundown would think there were many more of them than there truly were. Their real numbers and position remained unknown to the foe.'),
('4542', 'Creator of the Brew of Individual Senses and the Brew of the Four Senses.$b$bZhen Yun, whose other senses were sharpened by his blindness, suffered for many years from the taste of ordinary pandaren ale, which he considered thoroughly vulgar. After long years of wandering and experimentation, he invented two brews that delighted all four of his senses, but having perfected the two separately, he could not combine them into one that he would call perfect. He spent his whole life searching, until one day he uncovered the secret of a recipe that allowed him to unite the two drinks into one: the Brew of the Four Senses.'),
('4546', 'Ten thousand years ago, Shaohao, the last emperor of Pandaria, used the power of these sacred waters to save his land from the Sundering that destroyed the rest of the world.$b$b"At the sunset of the final day, the sky blazed with green fire, and the earth shook with terror. But the emperor was not afraid. His mind was clear, free of doubt and despair. He feasted and sang while the sky split apart above him."$b$b"Seeing fear and doubt in the eyes of his subjects, Emperor Shaohao proclaimed: Every day must be lived to the fullest, and every night slept with peace kept in one''s soul."'),
('4554', 'Legend says he descended to the Terrace of Endless Spring to separate Pandaria from the rest of the world, but his strength failed him: the land shuddered, but did not move. He doubted himself, and then the Sha of Doubt advanced upon him from the east. The emperor grew afraid, and the Sha of Fear burst from its bonds in the west. In despair, Shaohao called upon the Jade Serpent.$b$bAnd the emperor heard words of wisdom from the Jade Serpent: "Pandaria is not only the Pandaren Empire. Your enemies in the west are as much a part of this land as your empire beyond the wall."'),
('4555', 'Seeing that everything around him had become one, and that the land he loved so dearly was no longer merely the Pandaren Empire, Shaohao at last understood. His staff fell, and his spirit merged with the land. When the world was torn to pieces, Pandaria quietly drifted out into the ocean. And when the emperor''s empty robes settled upon the floor, an impenetrable veil of mist surrounded the land, hiding it from the outside world.'),
('4557', 'Ten thousand years ago, on this very spot, Shaohao, the last emperor of Pandaria, defeated the Sha of Anger, the Sha of Hatred, and the Sha of Violence.$b$bAn excerpt from the Book of Burdens, Chapter 19:$b$b"Emperor Shaohao, guided by fearlessness and self-confidence, believed he had no equal. But on the advice of the Red Crane, he sought counsel from the White Tiger, spirit of strength."$b$b"The White Tiger saw in Shaohao the recklessness that so often accompanies those who know no fear, and which can destroy them. He gathered the greatest warriors of Pandaria to test the emperor."$b$b"Shaohao was given a ten-foot staff and told to strike at least one of the warriors. The fight went on for hours, but the warriors were too swift and nimble for the not-so-skilled emperor. He raged, he cursed, and at last he broke his staff over his knee."'),
('4558', 'Humbled, the emperor asked the White Tiger what his mistake had been, and learned that his weakness lay in his own passions. To save Pandaria, Shaohao had to face his own anger, hatred, and violence in battle.$b$bThe Monkey King set to work and carved three masks. The emperor donned each in turn, and with the help of his friends, and of all the greatest warriors of Pandaria, the Sha of Anger, the Sha of Hatred, and the Sha of Violence were defeated and imprisoned in the depths of the earth.$b$bForever changed, the emperor set out on his final journey, in which he found patience, love, and peace.'),
('4560', 'The celebrated pandaren explorer Liu Lang was born and raised here in Stoneplow, on a small farm not far from this very spot.$b$bHe was supposed to help his family with the farm work, but instead he often climbed the cliffs to the south and gazed out at the boundless expanse of the ocean. "I want to see what lies beyond the sea," he announced one day, to the great amusement of his peers (it was widely believed that the rest of the world had perished in the Sundering).$b$bAt first Liu Lang wanted to sail in a boat, but the local fishermen told him he would never be able to find his way home through the veil of mist.$b$bLater he declared that he would explore the world on the back of a sea turtle, because turtles always return to their native shores.$b$bAfter that, the locals suspected that Liu Lang had eaten some rotten turnips and lost his mind.'),
('4563', 'From this very spot, many centuries ago, carrying only an umbrella and a knapsack of food, the young pandaren explorer Liu Lang set out to discover the world on the back of a sea turtle.$b$bIn those days everyone believed that only Pandaria had survived the Sundering. And everyone thought Liu Lang was... "not quite right" in the head.$b$bBut they realized they had been wrong when, five years later, Liu Lang returned and told them of mysterious lands found far beyond the seas. And until the end of his days he returned to Pandaria every five years, while his turtle grew and grew, until it became so enormous that an entire settlement was built on its back.$b$bSeized by wanderlust, the pandaren often gazed out to sea, awaiting Liu Lang''s return, and even in our day, if someone stares into the distance for long, they are asked: "Waiting for the turtle?"'),
('4564', 'The mogu failed to create an obedient army from the saurok, and so they had to devise other ways to build the perfect war machine. Using dark magic of unknown origin, they performed rituals to subjugate the souls of others and bind them in bodies of stone.$b$bDynasties came and went, while an enormous army of these living statues piled up in a vast underground vault in the Kun-Lai mountains.$b$bThe pandaren monks knew of this secret weapon and feared its might. They managed to seize the vaults just after the uprising began. The mogu were caught off guard by a swift force that swept down upon them from the Peak of Serenity. The battle for the vaults lasted four days, but in the end a snowstorm drove the mogu from the mountain.$b$bDeprived of their secret weapon, the mogu were forced to fight the rebels on nearly equal terms.'),
('4567', 'Whatever you are $gbusy:busy; with, put it aside, $n!$b$bThe Horde and its vicious monkeys are preparing to attack.$b$bThey greatly outnumber us, so we cannot give them the chance to train their soldiers.$b$bDrop whatever you are doing and hurry to Pearlfin Village.$b$b- Admiral Taylor'),
('4593', 'Greetings, $gtraveler:traveler;!$b$bIt was no small effort to learn your name, and I am glad my message has found its recipient after all.$b$bAll is quiet in Pandaria - for now, but events in this world unfold quickly. Help us turn the brewing conflict to our advantage, and you will be $grewarded:rewarded; handsomely.$b$bGo to the Hidden Stair and find the tavern called "In the Mists." I will be waiting for you there.$b$b      The Black Prince'),
('4596', 'Mark''s warhorse galloped up to the building. Seeing him, one of the guards, whom nature had not shortchanged in curves, blushed faintly. Mark dismounted and handed the reins to the stablemaster who hurried over. Then he spoke, resting a hand on her shoulder. "Kama, have you considered my offer?" he asked with feigned seriousness.\n\nKama rolled her eyes. The black fur around them accentuated her grimace. "I don''t think the love of my life would take to it."\n\nMark burst out laughing and headed for the exit. "Even so, I would be glad to get to know her better some day!" he said as he left.'),
('4597', 'Contrary to custom, the tavern "In the Mists" was unusually popular with the locals that day, and Mark had to push through the crowd to a secluded spot in a dark corner of the room. "Mark, how glad I am to see you again," he heard a silky, almost purring voice say.\n\nMark smiled and squinted to let his eyes adjust to the gloom. "Madam Goya, what an honor!" he replied.\n\nMadam Goya curtsied, and Mark felt a rush of heat. He instantly remembered why they call pandaren women "dumplings." He bowed to her and gallantly took her paw. As he kissed her smooth fur, he kept his eyes on the guard with a scar between his eyes who stood behind Madam Goya.'),
('4598', '"Mark, would you agree to a... special offer? I fear I cannot fill your usual order today." It did not escape Mark''s notice that she stressed the word "usual" and spoke it with a note of disappointment. Their eyes met again, and she squeezed his hand tightly before letting it go.\n\n"Well, if you insist, at least let me gauge what you have to offer me," he said.\n\n"I have excellent armor, a charming companion, and even a rare mount," replied Madam Goya. Her usual playfulness had returned to her voice. She fell silent for a moment and propped her chin on her paw, feigning thought. "But the most valuable thing I have is tucked away upstairs."\n\nMark raised his eyebrows, the very picture of interest. "Then I''ll see you upstairs," he announced his decision. Her lashes fluttered as Mark headed for the stairs leading to the second floor of this strange tavern.'),
('4599', 'In the room upstairs Mark found two girls so beautiful that he could barely keep his feet. One had long blond hair that seemed like gazing straight into the Sunwell. The other had short hair, black as night. Shaking off his daze, Mark realized he was face to face with enemies! He drew his sword from its scabbard, and its glow lit the faces of the blood elves.\n\nThe elf with the shining hair spoke first. "Well, look at that. Always ready for a fight." She touched the tip of the sword with a finger and moved the blade away from herself, stepping closer without taking her eyes from his. "Do you believe in love at first sight? Or do I need to walk by again?"\n\nMark leaned toward her, pressing the sword blade against her body, whispered something in her ear, and drew back, waiting for a reaction to his words.'),
('4600', '"No. I won''t do that... But my sister wouldn''t mind!" the blonde replied, and laughed. The dark-haired elf arched a brow, nodded, and shrugged. She made a barely noticeable gesture with her hand, and her body filled with heat. Her clothes, which were already far too revealing, burned away at once. As Mark took her in his arms, she whispered some barely audible words. A sigil appeared above Mark''s head, surrounding him with a glow, though he could not see it.\n"How... good I feel. What was that?" he asked.\n\n"Fortitude, my lord. You will need it." Only then did he notice that the elf''s feet did not touch the floor and that he felt no weight from her. Mark''s head spun, countless possible courses of events whirling in his mind, and his strong arms...\n\n<The remaining pages are shrouded in mist.>'),
('4608', 'Two wise monks met at the Temple of the Jade Serpent to discuss the nature of truth. The surface of the water looked calm and serene... and they decided to hire a fisherman to take them out to sea so they could admire the beautiful view during their engrossing conversation. "A calm like this comes only before a storm," the fisherman warned them, but the monks trusted only their own eyes and insisted.$b$b"Truth is absolute," said the first monk as the boat put out to open water. "There is but one truth in the universe, and we must work tirelessly to know it."'),
('4609', '"Truth is relative!" the second retorted, as clouds had already begun to gather above them. "There are many truths in the universe, and only a fool would think that there is one truth for all."$b$bThe fisherman kept silent while the monks argued, but one of them asked for his opinion. "I have not given it any thought," he admitted. Both monks shook their heads reproachfully and clucked their tongues. Then they found out that the fisherman could not even read or write. "You have wasted your life," they said.'),
('4621', 'Banana totem.$b$bThing best no more!$b$bGrok all shnurapses!'),
('4622', 'Hozen-fu skuttle:$b$bGrab, chik, chew dooker.$b$bRepeat til grok all-all.'),
('4623', 'Forget not the fields aflame.$b$bForget not our might.$b$bRemember the brave whom we served,$b$bAnd the lands scorched in this war.$b$b"The yaungol are coming!" they lamented,$b$bWhile their voices could still be heard and the cries had not died.$b$b"Yaungol, yaungol!" - they expected no mercy,$b$bFrozen with fear, vanishing in the fire.'),
('4649', 'At that moment lightning split the sky, thunder rolled, and a mighty downpour began. The little boat quickly filled with water and capsized. The fisherman threw off his cloak and swam for shore.$b$b"Help us! We cannot swim!" cried the monks as the waves closed over them. "You have wasted your lives," the fisherman answered.'),
('4690', 'In the 170th year of the Thunder King''s reign, the spellbinders of the Korun clan came to Lei Shen with their greatest creation.\nIt was a bell, cast from the flesh of its makers, forged with the fire of the stars and tempered in the breath of utter darkness. The sound of this bell could shake the whole world and reach the heavens.\nIn battle, the thunderous tolling of the bell inspired Lei Shen''s warriors. It filled their hearts with fury and hatred, making them stronger on the field. In the hearts of the Emperor''s enemies, the piercing peal sowed fear and doubt, driving them to flee.\nStanding in awe of this creation, the Thunder King called it the "voice of the gods" and named it Shenqing, the Divine Bell.'),
('4770', 'Many years after the age of the titans came to an end, the mogu fell under the curse of flesh and began to kill one another. Chaos reigned in the world. Mogu warlords fought for power and new lands. It was the Era of the Hundred Kings.$b$bThese were the days of Lei Shen. A young and proud mogu, he saw the fruits of his ancestors'' labor turn to dust on the war-ravaged land. All his life he had felt that his kin lived without purpose and gave no thought to their destiny in this world. $b$bThe titans were silent, and Lei Shen resolved that he would speak in their stead.'),
('4818', 'Here lies the dread Captain Genest, scourge of the Southern Seas.'),
('4884', 'An arakkoa has come to us - a sun priestess. She hungers to seize our lands, and she brings with her ash, wind, and death. She scorches our fields with sunfire.'),
('4885', '<Several pages are missing.>'),
('4886', 'Our village is slowly dying. Many of our defenders have been struck down by a terrible curse that we call "sunsickness."$B$BShaken and ravaged, barely able to stand, they return to the village unable to see the Light or feel its grace.'),
('4887', 'Today in the forest near our village I came upon a sun priestess. I managed to escape, but she had time to afflict me with sunsickness.$B$BEver since she spoke that monstrous spell, I have been praying to the Light... but I feel nothing.$B$BWhere there was once sacred and endless grace, there is now only emptiness.$B$BI have never been so afraid.'),
('4888', 'I managed to send the children, the sick, and the elderly away from here. Perhaps beyond the river they will be safe.$B$BOnly I remain.$B$BToday I will kill the sun priestess.'),
('4889', '<Most of the remaining pages have been torn out, except for the last.>'),
('4890', 'A long time has passed since I killed the feathered witch. How many years? I can no longer count.$B$BI am an old man, and I have little time left.$B$BFor so many years I wandered blind and alone, deprived of the Light. What good is an avenger who cannot deliver vengeance?$B$BIt is like a delicacy without taste. Teaching without understanding. Life without meaning.$B$BThe darkness beckons me. The world grows darker. Without the Light, I have no hope.'),
('4917', 'The path to the secret lava pools is definitely not here'),
('4918', 'Only the secret lava pool is definitely here\n\nStand here'),
('4949', 'This is incredible!\n\nI managed to unearth an artifact of unknown origin. The moment I touched it, images of every magnaron, gronn, and their kind that live in Gorgrond filled my mind.\n\nMore than that, I was overcome with the feeling that all these creatures are an extension of me. I could communicate with them, manipulate them, and even control their thoughts.\n\nI am returning to the foundry to report everything to Blackhand in person. This discovery will help us cleanse Draenor of our enemies faster - and then many other worlds as well!'),
('4973', 'Day 0 \nArrived at the coast; most of our equipment is intact. Finn assures us this is the perfect spot. Garris and Claudia keep bickering after the recent arakkoa attack. Ephial keeps going on about "ley lines" (I don''t know what those are). We can''t even say exactly when we came through the portal - autumn, maybe? I call this day day zero.'),
('4974', 'Day 2\nAbout a dozen lumberjacks have vanished. The one who came back had a wasp stinger as long as my arm sticking out of his belly. Dunberlin and Ephial proposed going out there with a war party and clearing the place out. I sent them off with a couple of companions. We badly need timber.'),
('4975', 'Day 4\nVindicator Maraad paid us a visit. He is pleased with our progress. He suggested we send workers to the draenei city of Elodor for food. I refused. There is no way I''m sending my best workers halfway across the world for a bit of food. I decided on a different course. Madam Goya, that friendly pandaren, has offered us whole wagonloads of provisions at a reasonable price. She seems trustworthy.'),
('4976', 'Day 7\nAn Iron Horde attack! Thank the Light we had time to raise the walls. Dunberlin fought like a wild beast. And yet we lost many good companions in the battle, many wounded in the back. Ephial is still nowhere to be seen, though hours have passed since the attack. Something is wrong here. I gave the workers a day off to bury the dead. Work will continue tomorrow. The barracks are almost ready. A little more and we can start on the armory.'),
('4977', 'Day 12\nToday Sir Garris arrived at the town hall together with the Black Prince Wrathion. It was hard to say which of them had taken the other prisoner. Wrathion seeks refuge in my garrison, says he angered the ogres. Garris declared that Wrathion fled from the Alliance. Lady Claudia arrived with her squad, and the fighters fanned out and drew their weapons. The situation grew tense. I told Wrathion we would be glad to have him as our "guest" on condition that he remain under house arrest and round-the-clock guard. A message has been sent to the King. A Black Dragon has taken shelter in my garrison - what is there to fear now?'),
('4978', 'Day 15\nThe barracks are finished. Work on the armory is in full swing. This morning a large shipment of assorted materials arrived - a "gift" from Wrathion for building the tavern. How does he manage it? I went to talk to Wrathion, and he told me to keep an eye on Ephial. I trust neither of them.'),
('4979', 'Day 16\nTook a couple of guards aside and offered them a sizable bonus to watch Wrathion around the clock. They dropped their eyes and confessed that Wrathion is already paying them to watch ME. Well, how about that...'),
('4980', 'Day 21 \nA letter has come from Gargtok, inviting us to a contest in the Blood Ring. Here is a chance to show everyone what the Alliance can do. I''ll give the prize money to Goya. Set out with Garris and Claudia to "win it all for the glory of Rynn." Left Dunberlin to guard the garrison.'),
('4982', 'Day 25\nVictory! Garris fought brilliantly in the arena.'),
('4993', 'Day 27\nReturned. The garrison is in chaos. Goya''s supplies never arrived. Dunberlin decided martial law is a fine thing. Now Ephial commands it. Wrathion is nowhere to be found. He took several of my best companions with him. Time to roll up my sleeves and set things right.'),
('5143', 'Today was a day of great shame. We plundered a whole heap of loot on the coast and, as usual, carried it to our cave.\n\nBut then everything went wrong. First Mate Thorglork decided his share was too small and talked part of the crew into mutiny. In the cave they attacked Captain Ironbeard and killed him! By the time we understood what had happened, it was too late. I did not want to share the captain''s fate, so for now I had to accept the new state of affairs...\n\nThorglork has taken Ironbeard''s place, and he passes himself off as him so that no one topside learns what happened. When I see his vile face, I want to split his skull with an axe - and then slit my own belly for not having put an end to this dishonor yet...'),
('5144', 'The invasion is in full swing. We are being transferred to Ironfist Harbor in Nagrand, so we are saying farewell to the Tanaan Jungle.\n\nI greased the palm of a warlock, and he promised to help. He says his magic will bring Ironbeard back... for the most part. So I will go to our cave and try to resurrect him. Maybe together we can still make things right.'),
('5145', 'The ritual failed... for the most part. We could not resurrect Ironbeard. That is, he came back, but he is still dead. His spirit remembers only the moment of his death, and he is enraged beyond all measure. I told him that Thorglork was now passing himself off as him, and he flew into such a rage that he attacked me on the spot. I barely got away.\n\nHis screams and curses against traitors are still ringing in my ears - it gives me goosebumps. So I will have to go back to the ship. Never mind. I''ll still think of a way to win back my lost honor...'),
('5159', 'DAY 1.$b$bThe forces of the Scourge are closing in on Pyrewood Village, and it seems we have nowhere to turn.$B$BThe gates of Gilneas remain closed, but at night we hear the howling of wild beasts from behind the walls. Rumor has it that some terrible evil is awakening in the city.$B$BI think we will find no safe haven behind the walls of Greymane either.'),
('5160', 'DAY 10.$b$bI have purchased a merchant ship, the Sea Wolf. She can carry forty passengers.$B$BI have laid in enough provisions and spoken with everyone who no longer wishes to stay here.$B$BTomorrow we sail for distant shores and a new life.'),
('5161', 'DAY 23.$b$bWe have set course south toward the city of Stormwind. The humans have founded a new capital, and we hope it will become our new home.$B$BFor now the sea is calm, but storm clouds are gathering on the horizon.'),
('5162', 'DAY 30.$b$bWe were caught in a terrible storm and fought the elements for a whole week. The ship has been thrown completely off course.$B$BAll around us is nothing but boundless sea. The weather is overcast. The stars are not visible at night, and we do not know where to sail.'),
('5163', 'DAY 47.$b$bThe sea shares its gifts generously with us, and hunger is no threat for now, but our supplies of fresh water are nearly gone. For the past several days there has been a dead calm, and the heat is simply exhausting.'),
('5164', 'DAY 53.$b$bTwo days ago we were caught in a storm again. The Sea Wolf is no more.$B$BOur fine ship ran onto a rocky shoal. Fortunately, everyone survived.$B$BWhen the storm died down, we looked around. We had been cast up on some utterly unfamiliar shore.$B$BA green plain stretched into the distance; somewhere far off a fortress rose, and nearby lay a vast, untouched forest.'),
('5165', 'DAY 54.$B$BWe could not save anything from the ship, but the land here is fertile and fit for plowing.$B$BThe forest is full of mushrooms and berries.$B$BThe old fortress looks long abandoned.$B$BWe have decided to settle here and found a new settlement, which will be called Bradensbrook.'),
('5189', 'Fjarnskaggl was not always rare, my child. It was an inseparable part of our culture; to be vrykul was to live alongside fjarnskaggl. We spread it on our beds, gave it to flavor our dishes; we dressed our children in it.\n\nNow it is dying. Today it grows only in rare patches on the edges of our city, and not in thick rows as in the old days, when we harvested it plentifully. Only the hardiest fjarnskaggl can survive the piercing winds of Stormheim, and the cold winters of Northrend are unbearable even for it.\n\nWe have lost our way, my daughter. We have forgotten where fjarnskaggl came from. And yet this grass is a gift from the heavens. Those who look down upon us from the heights of the sky want us to be happy, and fjarnskaggl is one of their gifts to us. To gather fjarnskaggl is to unbraid the beard of a god.'),
('5192', 'But there were times when we did not value the gift of the gods and took it for granted. We burned fjarnskaggl when we could have burned plain straw. We fed it to our cattle. We gathered the priceless grass and then let it rot in the barns.\n\nDo not repeat the mistakes of our ancestors, my child. Treat the sacred grass with due reverence, and perhaps it will return to us in all its former glory.'),
('5244', 'If you are disciplined and strong, enter. Trials await you.'),
('5253', 'I, Isgarogg, the last of the dragons, record the knowledge given to me, at the dictation of my master - Neltharion, the Earth-Warder of Azeroth.\n\nThis record is meant to commemorate our fiftieth attempt to unite the elements and create a binding ore. My master expects that it will help him ascend to the pinnacle of power, but years, even centuries, may pass before we obtain a stable substance.\n\nBelow is the refining recipe. We have established that it is an effective method, but the resulting substance requires additional processing.'),
('5322', 'Huginn and Muninn sat on a branch and watched their master. The mighty vrykul lounged below, reading the text on ancient tablets while eating ripe plums. Plum juice dripped onto the stone.\n\n"What does it say? What is he reading?" Muninn asked.\n\nHuginn twisted his head, trying to make out the runes on the stone below with one eye.\n\n"I can''t read!" croaked the larger raven. "You know that. Why are you teasing me?" Huginn clacked his beak in offense.\n\nMuninn relieved himself, narrowly missing the vrykul.\n\n"Let him make something useful for us. An eye, for example... caw!" Muninn would not let up.\n\nHuginn croaked in agreement, and then the vrykul, the only one who could understand what they were saying, looked at the ravens.\n\n"If you two will leave me in peace for a while, I will make a trinket with which you can read whatever your little bird minds desire. Now, quiet."\n\n...'),
('5323', 'Time passed, but the ravens felt its passing quite differently from mortals. They spent several centuries reading everything they could find with the priceless Raven''s Eye. For days on end they deciphered ancient runes, half worn away by time and weather, and read records in long-dead languages. And they were happy. Well, as happy as ravens can be. Actually, it is believed they are incapable of true happiness. They''re just ravens.\n\nBut one day a stranger came to them - a night elf, one of those new kaldorei. At least, everything pointed to it. These little folk had always brought nothing but trouble - not like the vrykul. But this guest seemed different from the others. And in his hand he held something that gave off an incredibly enticing sweet aroma - nothing like it had the ravens ever encountered.\n\n"What have you got there, my black-feathered friends?" asked the bearded kaldorei.'),
('5324', '"Caw!" he heard in reply. Only their master could have understood the birds'' words. But thanks to the Raven''s Eye, they understood the stranger''s speech perfectly. They had long since learned all the languages of the mortal peoples.\n\n"You know, the three of us have something in common, for my name is Ravencrest. You might say I am a successor of your kind," explained the night elf, and his voice softened in a wondrous way. "May I try to read what is written on the tablet? It looks tremendously interesting." With these words the elf drew cautiously near to examine the runes carved in the stone.\n\nHuginn and Muninn were bewitched by the aroma of the unknown treat that Ravencrest held in his hand. The ravens lifted their heads and could think of nothing but the mysterious delicacy, whatever it might be. They let the elf approach.\n\nWhat danger could this elf possibly pose?'),
('5325', 'The visitor raised an eyebrow, then frowned. He peered at the text on the tablet that the ravens had been reading; it was an amusing tale of a foolish boy and girl who were tricked, and then eaten, by a cunning vrykul witch.\n\n"Alas, I cannot read this. I am not familiar with the ancient runes of the noble vrykul," Ravencrest admitted. He sighed mournfully and let his shoulders drop. "I know that ravens are the cleverest creatures in all of Azeroth, but how do you know the language of this ancient text?" he asked with feigned surprise.\n\nMuninn, the more suspicious raven, glared angrily at the elf with his left eye and glanced only once or twice - well, perhaps three times - at the elf''s hand to make sure the mysterious object was still there.'),
('5326', 'Before the wary raven could stop his trusting brother, the latter had already given away their secret. Huginn tried three times to peck at their most prized treasure. However hard a raven''s beak may be, not a scratch appeared on the flawless surface of the magical gem.\n\n"Oh, what a marvel! Does this trinket really let you read what is written on the stone?" asked the cunning elf. Both birds stared at him, ruffling their feathers.\n\n"No, no, friends, you have misunderstood. I only wish to borrow the gem for a short while. Let me take it so that I may read the runes on the stone," said the elf, "and afterward we can discuss what is carved there together. In return, I can give you this."\n\nRavencrest at last revealed the object he had been hiding in his hand. To the ravens it was perhaps the most incredible thing they had ever seen. After Havi''s gem, of course.'),
('5327', 'The kaldorei unhurriedly set the elven sweet on a nearby stone. In confusion the ravens turned their heads, shifting their gaze from the gem to the elf to the treat. But soon they gave in to instinct and darted to the stone. The candy was incredibly delicious, tastier than anything they had tried before, including the food of the gods in the Halls of Valor. They pecked greedily at the delicacy, feeling on top of the world.\n\nWhen the treat was gone, the ravens raised their heads - perhaps their new friend would have more? But the elf was nowhere to be seen. Neither was the Raven''s Eye.\n\nIn despair, Huginn and Muninn began to caw loudly, straining their throats with all their might. Taking flight, they solemnly swore that someday they would take revenge on the cunning elf, and on his entire race besides.'),
('5415', 'Though I should have shown him respect. After all, he saved my father.\n\nIt happened during a sortie that Kur''talos led; the party found itself in the wilds far from the fortress. I was not there myself; I was told afterward: some enraged fel hunter fell upon my father and managed to disarm him. The creature would have torn him apart had Illidan not intervened. Stormrage proved a skilled sorcerer with exceptional reflexes; he defeated the demon and saved my father from a dreadful death.\n\nInstead of hastening to forget the incident, my father decided to commemorate the event. He proclaimed Illidan his personal magus, and at the same time made him the chief subject of every conversation. The Moon Guard sought his audience to discuss the military situation, as well as the nature of demons and the art of arcane magic. Soon Illidan won renown as a sorcerer of unseen power.'),
('5416', 'From then on he always fought shoulder to shoulder with my father. And yet I regarded him with distrust, and resolved to learn as much as I could about the strange mage who had so suddenly found himself in my family''s circle of trust. \n\nAnd what I learned did nothing to dispel my misgivings. Quite the opposite.'),
('5523', '<HTML><BODY>\n<p></p>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-rogue-kingslayers" align="center"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="160"/>\n<H3 align="center">The Kingslayers</H3>\n<BR/>\n<P>\nThese blades are a superb instrument of death. In the art of assassination they have no equal. But as long as they are with you, the blades will relentlessly strive to bend your mind to the will of their master, Kil''jaeden.\n<BR/><BR/>\nPerhaps one day you will be able to thank him for it in person.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5524', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Kingslayers, Part 1</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThese daggers were made to slay heroes and spill innocent blood, and in the hands of Garona Halforcen they drank their fill. \n<BR/><BR/>\nAnguish and Sorrow lived up to their names. They killed kings, commanders, soldiers, mages, and demons - their victims were beyond counting. With these weapons the first Horde nearly conquered Azeroth. \n<BR/><BR/>\nThe bloody path of these daggers began on Draenor, soon after the Burning Legion enslaved the orc clans.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5525', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Kingslayers, Part 2</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nImmediately after the founding of the Horde, Gul''dan took Garona under his wing. She was an outcast, a half-breed: orc and draenei blood flowed in her veins, and from birth she had had to fight for her life. She quickly learned to avoid needless fights and to silently kill persistent pursuers. \n<BR/><BR/>\nHaving bent her to his will, Gul''dan secretly began to train Garona in the craft of the assassin. She did not understand why he was doing it, and she hated him for his cruelty. But she obeyed... the half-orc was accustomed to doing whatever it took to survive.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSoon Garona proved her mastery of every kind of weapon, but Gul''dan wanted more. His position in the Horde was precarious - should anyone learn that the Shadow Council was killing Gul''dan''s rivals, things would go badly for him. \n<BR/><BR/>\nGul''dan sought a weapon that would allow Garona to kill without leaving a trace.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5526', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Kingslayers, Part 3</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nGul''dan''s difficulty was resolved by the first warchief of the Horde. Blackhand and his clan owned the finest forges, and the smiths of Blackrock were famed among the orcs of Draenor. Trying not to attract undue attention, Gul''dan asked the smiths how their ancestors had managed to create the legendary Doom Hammer.\n<BR/><BR/> \n"We received it as a gift from the burning heart of Draenor," they answered.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThis was not the answer Gul''dan had hoped for. He wanted to put an end to the worship of the elements in the Horde, and he feared that turning to the spirits of rage for help would only strengthen the tradition. Instead, Gul''dan called upon Kil''jaeden, begging for the Legion''s help.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe demon lord agreed that the Shadow Council needed a silent and unnoticed killer. He gave Gul''dan detailed instructions... and all the materials to craft two deadly blades, the equal of which Draenor had never seen.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5527', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Kingslayers, Part 4</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nGul''dan''s request intrigued Warchief Blackhand. The warlock asked him to forge two daggers from a mysterious ore never seen before. In addition, he wanted to imbue them with some power that would reveal itself only at the proper moment. Blackhand agreed to forge the weapons himself in his own forge.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAs he quenched the blades, he sensed a dark power filling the daggers with unspeakable might. It was not the fury of the elements... the primal hatred of Kil''jaeden entered the unknown metal of the blades. \n<BR/><BR/>\nBlackhand felt that the daggers seemed to writhe in agony, which only the blood of new victims could soothe, and he named them Anguish and Sorrow. \n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5528', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Kingslayers, Part 5</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe power of the daggers delighted Gul''dan. Not only did the dark might of the Legion''s lord seethe within them: the enchantments laid upon the daggers suppressed the will of whoever held them.\n<BR/><BR/>\nTo test the new weapon, Gul''dan placed the daggers in Garona''s hands and gave an order. She obeyed... The unfortunate Shadow Council acolyte did not even have time to raise his hands: Garona cut his throat with a single precise stroke. \n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5529', 'The death was not a pleasant one. But it was swift, quiet, and efficient. When the orc breathed his last, the wound that had proved fatal took on an unusual appearance. No one could have said for certain whether he died by knife, axe, or spear. Gul''dan saw at once the possibilities this opened: mysterious deaths would sow discord in the enemy camp, and all suspicion would fall on whomever he chose. \n<BR/><BR/>\nTruly, priceless daggers!\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5530', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Kingslayers, Part 6</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the Horde went to war against the draenei, Gul''dan often made use of Garona''s services. Influential orcs who doubted him or disagreed with him were found dead after skirmishes with the draenei. The wounds, it seemed, were not made by orcish weapons, so no one suspected Gul''dan. \n<BR/><BR/>\nNor Garona. She was a half-breed, so most orcs simply did not notice her, and those who did considered her no more than Gul''dan''s servant, kept on a short leash. \n<BR/><BR/>\nHer quiet work helped the Horde defeat the draenei, and her blades helped keep control in the turmoil after the war. Through the fault of the Fel, life on Draenor vanished, but the tense situation in the Horde could still be managed. \n<BR/><BR/>\nGul''dan gave the orders, Garona carried them out. Her daggers knew no rest.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5531', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Slayers of Kings, Part 7</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nFor many years, Garona did the bidding of the Shadow Council. She obeyed them to survive, appeasing them with the blood of their enemies. She often dreamed of killing her masters with these daggers, but when Garona awoke, her mind, enslaved by sorcery, buried those thoughts in its deepest recesses.\n<BR/><BR/>\nBut after Gul''dan fell in the Tomb of Sargeras, the magical shackles binding Garona were broken as well. And now, more than anyone, she is glad to see these daggers deal out vengeance, destroying the demons of the Burning Legion.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5532', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Slayers of Kings, Part 8</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the Horde invaded Azeroth, Gul''dan ordered Garona to find the sorcerer Medivh and keep watch over him. In time, Garona would reveal herself to Khadgar, hoping that the humans would help her escape from Gul''dan.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHad she known that these daggers helped the warlock control her, she would simply have gotten rid of them, and everything might have turned out very differently. In the end, though Garona tried to resist, Gul''dan forced her to kill the King of Stormwind, Llane Wrynn.\n<BR/><BR/>\nFrom that day on, Garona was branded a traitor. Against her will, she returned to the Horde, and the daggers were with her.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5533', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Slayers of Kings, Part 9</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe Horde did not welcome Garona with honors. Warchief Blackhand had been slain by Orgrim Doomhammer at the Mak''gora. Gul''dan lay in oblivion, and the Shadow Council had vanished without a trace. Garona was tortured in the hope of learning where its members had gone.\n<BR/><BR/>\nWhen the Horde''s armies marched out to deal with the human-led Alliance, Garona escaped. She wandered Azeroth in search of a new life. All she had were her blades and her instinct for survival.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe wretches the Horde sent to hunt her down never stood a chance.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5534', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Slayers of Kings, Part 10</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nDuring the Second War, Garona began to realize how much power these blades held over her. Though she was now far from Gul''dan, she could feel the warlock trying to bend her will to his once more.\n<BR/><BR/>\nShe turned for help to the risen mage Meryl Winterstorm and asked him to hide the blades where no one could reach them. Garona wanted to make certain that no one else would fall victim to these weapons and become a plaything in the hands of the cunning warlock.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAnd so the daggers were consigned to darkness and oblivion for decades.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5535', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Slayers of Kings, Part 11</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nNot once in the entire history of these blades have they been used for glorious deeds. Never have they shone in the hands of valiant victors. Their purpose is to cause pain and suffering, and the harm these weapons have done to Draenor and Azeroth cannot be put into words.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSuch power must be treated with reverence. Do not forget how many innocent lives these blades have taken.\n<BR/><BR/>\nMay the Burning Legion pay for every drop of blood spilled by their unwilling wielder.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5536', '<HTML><BODY>\n<p></p>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-rogue-dreadblades" align="center"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="170"/>\n<H3 align="center">The Dreadblades</H3>\n<BR/>\n<P>\nAs you may have guessed, these blades were not forged on Azeroth. Handle them with great care, for they were made to enslave weak minds and those consumed by greed. Though their creator was cast into the Twisting Nether, he may yet return and bend your will to his own. Stay vigilant.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5537', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 1</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAt first, the Bloodsail pirates had no idea of the consequences awaiting them. They thought they had managed to steal a fragment of forbidden power from right under the demons'' noses. But their certainty that they would never have to pay for this "gift" proved a fatal mistake.\n<BR/><BR/>\nFor it was no accident that this weapon fell into the hands of Admiral Bloodsail. It was all planned by one of the Burning Legion''s commanders, who had many times before corrupted fools and turned them against their own worlds.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAnd here on Azeroth, that dreadful mission fell to the Dreadblades.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5538', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 2</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThese blades were created by an eredar named Talgat. It is not known exactly when he began studying Azeroth and its inhabitants, but it was certainly no coincidence that he fashioned a pair of blades that caught the eye of a pirate captain.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAfter all, Talgat''s role in the Legion was to help Kil''jaeden spread corruption among the peoples of many worlds. Countless civilizations had fallen at his command, and so he had mastered the art of tempting mortals. Sometimes he found potential victims among revered heroes, but more often they were those who lived on the fringes of society.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5539', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 3</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nTalgat had found a very effective method of corruption: bring together the selfish, the greedy, and the ruthless, promise them unheard-of power, and set them to compete against one another for it. In this way he could not only identify the strongest of the contenders, but also lead them astray. Swept up in the struggle for the prize, the victors never stop to think what it will cost them to win it, until it is too late.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe pirates of Azeroth seemed the perfect targets for such tactics. They hunted for treasure and then stole it from one another, and they cared little for risk.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIt is hardly surprising that, in choosing a suitable weapon, Talgat settled on light, agile blades like the sabers so prized by the sea''s raiders. With their help, the eredar master had little trouble luring the pirates over to the Legion''s side.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5540', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 4</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThese blades appeared on Azeroth several decades ago. A few sailors from Alliance ships told of escaping a band of pirates whose captain wielded shimmering blades that gave off black smoke. It soon became clear that those sailors had been incredibly lucky. The pirates, empowered by some unknown force, hunted Alliance vessels, and once they had boarded a ship, they left no one alive.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHigh Admiral Daelin Proudmoore outfitted a fleet to hunt down the marauders, and weeks later his ships managed to surround the pirate vessel off the coast of the Eastern Kingdoms. The pirates seemed possessed: nothing human remained in them. But after a concerted bombardment, their ship, the Ringleader, was sent to the bottom of the sea.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe admiral returned to his homeland believing that all the pirates had perished. But one had survived.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5541', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 5</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe only one to escape the sinking pirate ship was a young man hired onto the crew, who had seen with his own eyes his shipmates sink into an abyss of corruption and madness. He had gone to sea with them only because he needed the coin, so the unbridled bloodlust that seized the captain and the whole crew filled him with true horror.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSomething compelled the lad to carry the captain''s blades away with him, but when he heard an otherworldly whisper in his head, he had the good sense to resist its entreaties. He took the weapons and the treasure to his crew''s old lair on a remote island and buried it all as deep as he could. He believed these things were cursed.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe blades lay in the earth for many years.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5542', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 6</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the tectonic plates shifted during the Cataclysm, the Dreadblades surfaced once more. One day, a Bloodsail pirate ship with a small but fearsome crew put in at one of the tiny islands scattered across the sea to take shelter from an approaching storm. When the tempest had calmed somewhat, the pirates'' captain, Admiral Elisa Bloodsail, noticed something lying in the sand washed bare by the waves.\n<BR/><BR/>\nDrawing closer, they found gold, gems, and old weapons. It was the final cache of the Ringleader, one no one had ever suspected.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe buccaneers gleefully took all the treasure. Admiral Bloodsail kept a pair of gleaming blades for herself, for of all the weapons in the cache, only they had neither rotted nor rusted. And that decision proved fatal for her crew.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5543', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 7</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen Admiral Bloodsail took up the blades, a vision came to her: she commanded a vast pirate fleet, one that could conquer all the seas and all the peoples of Azeroth. Every ship that dared defy her would be put to the torch; every city that resisted her would be wiped from the face of the world.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIntoxicated by the vision, she reveled in every moment of that phantom glory. When she came to, Bloodsail ordered her crew to set course for the hideout of another band of pirates. The Bloodsail pirates, corruption spreading through their souls, burst in and slaughtered everyone in their path.\n<BR/><BR/>\nNot so much for plunder as for sport. The killing gave them genuine pleasure. The evil contained in the blades had taken root in their minds, and the last hope of salvation faded away.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5544', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 8</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe buccaneers, led by Captain Bloodsail, became the scourge of the southern seas, an unseen and inescapable danger lying in wait for sailors. Talgat, the blades'' creator, knew the risks of attracting too much attention, so at his command Admiral Bloodsail''s crew attacked only lone ships and did everything possible to keep them from escaping.\n<BR/><BR/>\nFor several years the pirates obediently followed their orders. They killed without mercy, and the power of the blades grew. When the other Bloodsail pirates learned of the madness that had befallen this crew, they tried to intervene. Bloodsail''s crew repelled the counterattack and seized the most valuable ship of the Bloodsail pirates, the Blood Veil.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5545', 'Soon the members of the crew began to change: their bodies withered, and they came to resemble walking skeletons.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAdmiral Bloodsail did not care. The blades thirsted for blood, and she killed to quench their thirst, and her own as well.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5546', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 9</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nShortly before the Legion''s invasion, Admiral Bloodsail at last learned the true power of her weapons. Talgat revealed himself to her and told her that she held only a paltry sliver of the Burning Legion''s might. But if she aided the demons in the coming war, she would gain immortality and know a power she had never dared dream of.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe admiral agreed without hesitation. She struck a bargain with Talgat, handing the souls of her crew into his keeping.\n<BR/><BR/>\nShe had already become an unseen terror plaguing the seas, and she reveled in it. The prospect of reducing an entire world to ruins consumed her thoughts.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5547', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 10</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the Legion struck, so did Admiral Bloodsail. It is now common knowledge that her crew destroyed several convoys of Horde and Alliance ships before they could reach the shores of the Broken Isles.\n<BR/><BR/>\nFortunately, Fleet Admiral Tethys, leader of the Bloodsail pirates, began to seek help. Had Admiral Bloodsail not been stopped, there is no telling how much more harm she would have done to the Legion''s opponents.\n<BR/><BR/>\nNow that threat is behind us.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5548', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">The Dreadblades, Part 11</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nTalgat, creator of the Dreadblades, has been defeated. His spirit has gone to the Twisting Nether, where it awaits its return.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAnd Talgat no longer holds his former power over the blades. The madness he breathed into his creation has weakened, and the insatiable thirst for blood has faded. Yet Talgat may try to regain his former dominion over them.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThen again, he may abandon the effort. After all, he has ample reason to fear the one who wields them now.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5549', '<HTML><BODY>\n<p></p>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-rogue-fangsofthedevourer" align="center"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="150"/>\n<H3 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer</H3>\n<BR/>\n<P>\nBe careful with these daggers. The assassin Akaari paid a terrible price for the right to wield them. They have carried out countless secret, silent killings across innumerable worlds, yet none of their wielders has managed to keep free will.\n<BR/><BR/>\nYou could become one of the deadliest killers in the entire universe. But take care.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5550', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 1</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThese daggers may, in their time, have passed through the hands of a skilled weaponsmith, but it was not he who imbued them with their deadly power. No. They were originally fangs torn from the maw of a slain fel hound: ancient, yet perfectly preserved. That creature was far deadlier than any other fel hound that ever ravaged this world. In all likelihood, the beast was a pet of Azeroth''s greatest enemy, Sargeras.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHe must have cared for it greatly, in his own way, of course. The fallen titan had granted it razor-sharp teeth saturated with dark energy, teeth that could pierce reality itself. How many innocent lives did these terrifying fangs claim? We will never know.\n<BR/><BR/>\nBut we do know the name of that nightmarish creature. Sargeras named his hound Bloodmaw, the Devourer.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5551', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 2</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhenever the Legion invaded another world, Bloodmaw often accompanied the demons, tearing enemy warriors to shreds. After battles, the eredar who tended the fel hound gathered the remnants of dark energy seeping from its fangs and used them to create powerful weapons and reagents.\n<BR/><BR/>\nBloodmaw met its end in a conquered world where the leaders had bowed to the Legion and no heroes remained willing to carry on the fight. But a young inhabitant of the dying world, filled with righteous fury, crept into the Legion''s fortress and killed several eredar guards, and Bloodmaw along with them, while they all slept. Seeing his pet dead, Sargeras flew into a rage. That world and all its inhabitants were reduced to dust and scattered across the Great Dark Beyond. Still, that fate was, if anything, a mercy to them.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSo died Bloodmaw, but even in death it continued to serve the Legion.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5552', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 3</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe dreadlord Mephistroth believed that Bloodmaw could still be of great service to the Legion. He extracted the fangs from the fel hound''s maw and carried them to a demonic forge on the eredar homeworld, Argus. Preserving the fangs'' dark energy was fraught with difficulties, and Mephistroth did not dare undertake the work himself. His underlings, eager to distinguish themselves, were willing to take great risks. But the warped power of the fangs proved too dangerous. Piercing the minds of the test subjects, it caused unspeakable agony, and the dreadlord would simply give their souls to the forge and find replacements among his servants.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIn the end he succeeded. The fangs were honed to a point, with their dark energy fully preserved.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5553', 'And so Mephistroth created the deadliest weapon in the entire universe. These daggers can warp space and bend light, concealing their wielder from the watchful eyes of the victim. Mephistroth presented the daggers to Sargeras, who was very pleased with the gift. Bloodmaw had inspired terror on the battlefield; now its fangs, in the hands of a skilled assassin, would become terror stalking from the shadows.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5554', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 4</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nIt took Sargeras a long while to find someone worthy of wielding the Fangs of the Devourer. Loyalty concerned him as well: skilled assassins are by nature treacherous and prone to betrayal. The very thought that Bloodmaw''s legacy might fall into the hands of the Legion''s enemies was unbearable to Sargeras.\n<BR/><BR/>\nImagine it: whoever used this weapon against the Legion would deal a personal insult to the lord of demons himself...\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5555', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 5</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAt last, Sargeras found one worthy to bear these daggers. Akaari, an eredar tracker, had proven herself during several of the Legion''s invasions, silently killing all who saw the fatal deception in the demons'' promises.\n<BR/><BR/>\nShe was summoned to Argus and told what an honor had been bestowed upon her. Sargeras promised that Akaari would become a fearsome instrument of death, one of the deadliest beings alive. The price? Akaari had to sacrifice her free will. Before she could be made a living weapon, every last chance of betrayal had to be eliminated.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIt was a terrible bargain, but Akaari accepted the offer.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5556', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 6</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe depths of Argus hold many secrets: there, in the gloom of underground chambers, the deadliest weapons can be forged and the strongest spirits broken. Akaari spent centuries there; her essence was reshaped, meticulously honed and interwoven with the energy of the daggers. Many eredar assassins were sacrificed to bind their souls to Akaari''s and grant her the experience of a hundred lives lived and the memory of a thousand killings.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe change that had once come over Bloodmaw now touched Akaari as well. She emerged from the underground chambers an instrument of death, without remorse and unquestioningly obedient to Sargeras. He knew the daggers were in safe hands, for Akaari''s hands obeyed his will.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAnd those hands carried out the death sentence for all who stood against the Burning Legion.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5557', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 7</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nA small but warlike world caught Sargeras''s attention. The Legion was sure to face fierce resistance there. In time, the inhabitants of this world could have been defeated by brute force, but Sargeras chose more refined means: fear and paranoia. This was Akaari''s first assignment since gaining her new abilities. A test of sorts. Alone, she infiltrated fortresses.\n<BR/><BR/>\nWithin a month, the world had descended into utter chaos. Tales of an unseen killer spread like wildfire; heroes and leaders died quietly, one after another, and the warriors split into factions, accusing one another of the notorious murders.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5558', 'When the Legion finally stood before them, they surrendered without a fight. An eternity in the demons'' service seemed a lesser evil than a life of constant fear of falling to the blade of an assassin lurking in the shadows.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAkaari passed the test with brilliance. She returned to the Legion''s ranks to continue the fight, this time in the open.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5559', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 8</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAkaari proved an invaluable ally. With her weapons she could manipulate kings and other leaders like puppets. She could not only eliminate anyone, but also take their place, assuming their appearance and copying their mannerisms.\n<BR/><BR/>\nOf course, a world cannot collapse because of a single assassin. But one assassin like Akaari may well hasten its end. A dagger through the heart of the right person can decide the outcome of a battle and bring down an entire kingdom. A panicking army that has just lost its commander is far easier to destroy than an organized and united one.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe Legion was highly successful at corrupting mortals whose minds were not strong enough. But there were failures, too, and that was when Akaari stepped in. If anyone found the strength to resist temptation, Akaari''s blades quickly solved the problem.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5560', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 9</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAkaari was a valuable asset to Sargeras because of her absolute devotion. She no longer had a will of her own, but her cunning remained. She asked Sargeras to let her roam the Twisting Nether. Sargeras granted her wish, curious how it might serve the aims of the Burning Legion.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAkaari lurked among the soldiers of Sargeras''s army, rooting out discontent and eliminating overly ambitious demons who hoped to seize power. She mastered the art of interrogation, inflicting unimaginable suffering and pain on anything capable of screaming. She built a citadel in one of the dead worlds to hold prisoners there and extract valuable information.\n<BR/><BR/>\nOver time, however, Akaari''s actions bred rebellious sentiment within the Legion. The demons lived in fear, imagining that one day Akaari''s blades would come for them, too, and thoughts of escape began to creep into their minds.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5561', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 10</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the demons learned the location of Akaari''s citadel, she ostentatiously abandoned her refuge. The citadel became a mere empty symbol. Yet it had served its purpose. After a time, Akaari would begin appearing there again, incognito, only to prepare for especially difficult invasions or to interrogate particularly important prisoners.\n<BR/><BR/>\nShe returned to Sargeras with new skills and a new philosophy: fear is a double-edged dagger, a useful but not always reliable means of achieving one''s goals. True, an unseen killer provoked fear, but an unknown killer could lull all vigilance to sleep. One cannot prepare to meet a danger that, as far as one can tell, simply does not exist.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5562', 'Akaari devoted herself to a new goal: to become truly invisible. A silent killer... She carried on with her work.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAnd one day she met her doom in her perfectly guarded fortress. Surely she could never have imagined such an end.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5563', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Fangs of the Devourer, Part 11</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWe may never know what drove Akaari to sacrifice her own will in service to Sargeras. But there can be no doubt that she served him well.\n<BR/><BR/>\nShe knew every subtlety of fear and could strike it into the hearts of her victims. When she met the fearless along her way, she skillfully eliminated them. When intimidation became an unproductive method, she learned to kill without anyone noticing.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe Burning Legion once used all these skills to great effect. Now they serve its enemies.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHow Sargeras will answer that, only time will tell.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5564', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Author''s Note</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe research is proceeding at full speed! These texts contain an enormous amount of information, but I think I will decipher all of it. The only thing I may run short of is time!\n<BR/><BR/>\nCheck back when the next study is complete, and I will continue compiling this encyclopedia.\n</P>\n<BR/>\n<P align="right">\n Senior Archivist Filius Sparkwright\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5789', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H1 align="center">Tales of the Hunt</H1>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="27"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-hunter-cover" align="center" width="230" height="230"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="27"/>\n<p align="center">Recorded by Holt Thunderhorn of the Unseen Path.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5918', '<HTML><BODY>\n<p></p>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-hunter-titanstrike" align="center"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="140"/>\n<H3 align="center">Titanstrike</H3>\n<BR/>\n<P>\nThe brilliant keeper Mimiron created many firearms, but Titanstrike is undoubtedly his crowning achievement. It was his first rifle, and he designed it so that it could command the energy of thunderstorms.\n<BR/><BR/>\nMimiron presented the weapon to his good friend, the keeper Thorim, to use in battle. Thorim was a legendary warrior who loved the hunt. He learned to wield Titanstrike with perfect mastery. Its power was enough to bring down any beast, even the strongest and largest.\n<BR/><BR/>\nNow Titanstrike is in your hands. Let us hope that in you it has found a worthy successor to Thorim.\n<BR/><BR/>\nGood hunting. Aim true, and never miss.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5919', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 1</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe idea of Titanstrike did not originate in a mortal mind, nor was it brought to life by mortal hands. Its creator was a remarkable being named Mimiron. Mimiron was one of the keepers, a race of noble creatures who fought the Old Gods and ultimately imprisoned them deep beneath the earth.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSome of Mimiron''s kin could command storms. Others were mighty warriors. And Mimiron''s own strength lay in his exceptional mind. In ingenuity and intellect, none could match him.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5920', 'Day and night, Mimiron devised and built the most intricate mechanisms. He took particular interest in experimenting with different kinds of weapons that the keepers and their servants could use to defend Azeroth. When the Earthen asked Mimiron to invent something that could strike enemies from a distance, the keeper threw himself into the work.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSoon he presented the world with the first firearm in history: the rifle "Titanstrike."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5921', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 2</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nIn truth, Mimiron''s inventions seldom worked as intended. At least, not at first. His favored method was trial and error, which sometimes did not bode well for the welfare of his loyal mechanical servants.\n<BR/><BR/>\nOnce, while testing one of the first prototypes of Titanstrike, Mimiron started a fire in his workshop that injured more than a dozen poor mechagnomes. The incident taught the keeper a lesson. After repairing his injured servants, Mimiron set about fine-tuning the rifle''s mechanism. He went off to test the next prototype on a mountain peak, where no one would be hurt if something went wrong with the weapon again.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5922', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 3</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nMimiron''s decision to test Titanstrike on a mountaintop led, once again, to disaster, but at the same time gave him a brilliant idea. The new rifle had been made from a special alloy that, it turned out, accumulated static electricity. When Mimiron fired the rifle, lightning struck from a storm cloud hanging over the mountain, hit Titanstrike directly, and split it in two.\n<BR/><BR/>\nMimiron was neither angered nor disappointed in the slightest. On the contrary, he was jubilant. A new brilliant idea had been born in him. The keeper reassembled Titanstrike, this time paying special attention to its connection with the elements of the storm.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHe designed the rifle so that it could absorb and channel the energy of lightning.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5923', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 4</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAn excerpt from the historian Evelyn''s "Storm Thieves" (Chapter 4).\n<BR/><BR/>\n"To create Titanstrike, Mimiron required help. He needed to turn to the keeper Thorim.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIt was Thorim who held the crackling might of the heavens. His abode, the Temple of Storms, served as a conduit for thunder and lightning. It made an ideal forge for restoring Titanstrike.\n<BR/><BR/>\nOne day, as a storm raged above the temple, the two keepers set to work. Thorim battled the wind and lightning until he managed to subdue them. He gathered all the power of the storm and sealed it within an energy sphere called the Thunderspark. All that enormous energy was now contained within his temple.\n<BR/><BR/>\nMimiron had prepared ahead of time. He used the Thunderspark to charge Titanstrike. Lightning shook the temple''s vaults until the keeper succeeded in sealing the fearsome storm within his weapon."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5924', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 5</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAn excerpt from the historian Evelyn''s "Storm Thieves" (Chapter 11).\n<BR/><BR/>\n"The Earthen have a legend of the time when the ''sky went mad.'' They say that peals of thunder rolled in from the edges of Azeroth for days on end, for weeks. Many keepers thought something terrible had happened: that the sky itself had split. But Mimiron paid the noise no heed.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSeveral keepers, led by Loken, set out to find the source of the thunder. At last they found it: an island far to the south, home to devilsaurs and other scaled monstrosities. There the keepers found Thorim, hunting with his worgs, Hati and Skoll."\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5925', '"Loken ran to the keeper to warn him of the storm in the sky. Suddenly he realized that the cause of the storm was Thorim himself.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAlmost as soon as Titanstrike was ready, Mimiron gave it to Thorim. And Thorim immediately went off to test the weapon in his favorite hunting grounds. His own energy complemented the rifle''s explosive power. Every shot from it brought forth a thunderclap that echoed across the world and threatened to split the sky."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5926', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 6</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAn excerpt from the historian Evelyn''s "Storm Thieves" (Chapter 20).\n<BR/><BR/>\n"Only one beast ever escaped the great hunter Thorim and his worgs: the Jormungar worm. This monster, covered in thick plates of white stone, was the largest of its kind.\n<BR/><BR/>\nUpon seeing the worm, Thorim took aim at it with Titanstrike. The first shot cracked the Jormungar''s stone hide. But before Thorim could fire again, the worm lunged at him and knocked the rifle from his hands. Thorim fought the worm with his bare hands and forced it to burrow into the earth.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSo began a hunt that would last for decades. Traveling the northern lands, Thorim and his worgs tracked the worm and attacked it whenever it surfaced. In their final encounter, the keeper managed to land a shot between the plates of the monster''s armor. It has not been seen since."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5927', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 7</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nIn the end, betrayal put an end to the unity of the keepers. Most of them found themselves prisoners in their fortress of Ulduar. Crushed by grief, Thorim hid from the world in the Temple of Storms. Even hunting no longer brought him joy. Titanstrike was kept in his abode.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAges passed, and the servants of the titans spread across the icy expanses of the north. The vrykul united into clans eager to expand their domains.\n<BR/><BR/>\nBut a warrior named Volund aspired to an even bolder goal. He dreamed of becoming ruler of all the vrykul. And so he set out in search of the powerful machines and fearsome weapons created by the keepers. In his wanderings he came upon a lone mechagnome. The vrykul took the defenseless clockwork creature captive and forced it to tell of Mimiron''s works.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIt was from this mechagnome that he learned of Titanstrike and where it lay.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5928', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 8</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAfter Loken''s betrayal, Thorim withdrew to the Temple of Storms. Sinking into a restless slumber, he lay motionless for years on end, while Hati and Skoll roamed the forests without a master.\n<BR/><BR/>\nWaiting for just such an opportunity, Volund stole Titanstrike. He caught a mighty proto-drake and rode it up to the Temple of Storms. Snatching Titanstrike from right under Thorim''s nose, the daring vrykul fled.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5929', 'Waking to find it missing, Thorim called for Hati and Skoll. Picking up Volund''s trail, the worgs and their master gave chase. For now the vrykul was one step ahead of his pursuers, but he knew this could not last long. Thorim and his worgs were renowned for their hunting skill. In the end they would surely have caught up with Volund, and he would have gotten what he deserved.\n<BR/><BR/>\nSo Volund decided to leave the northern lands, taking with him the captive mechagnome and several relics he had found. He traveled far to the south, to a land he knew only by hearsay: Stormheim.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5930', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 9</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAn excerpt from Chapter 3 of the historian Llor''s work, "The Pretender Keeper."\n<BR/><BR/>\n"Boasting of his power, Volund demanded that the vrykul of Stormheim submit to him. But, of course, they refused. At least at first. Some vrykul tried to subdue the overconfident stranger. Volund killed them all with Titanstrike. Every shot thundered and shook everything around like the tread of a giant.\n<BR/><BR/>\nVolund''s weapon filled the vrykul with terror. Because it was the might of the keepers, the vrykul came to believe that those mythical beings favored him.\n<BR/><BR/>\nVolund proclaimed himself a great warlord and gathered the warriors of Stormheim under his banners. With his army he set out to conquer the neighboring lands and add them to the domains of the Stormheim vrykul."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5931', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 10</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAn excerpt from Chapter 7 of the historian Llor''s work, "The Pretender Keeper."\n<BR/><BR/>\n"Chief Volund had enough warriors and weapons to subdue the lands around Stormheim. But his camp lacked unity.\n<BR/><BR/>\nWhen the vrykul were warring with the mighty drogbar near Stormheim, suspicions awoke in Volund''s heart. He believed that his henchmen were plotting to kill him and take Titanstrike for themselves. After all, if he had stolen the weapon from a keeper, someone else could just as easily steal the artifact from him.\n<BR/><BR/>\nVolund''s paranoia drove him to torture and execute many of his allies. Most of the vrykul warriors abandoned the warlord as he lost his mind, and his once-fearsome army came to nothing."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5932', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Titanstrike, Part 11</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nMany years later, sensing death approaching, Volund ordered his enslaved mechagnome and the few remaining vrykul followers to bury him along with Titanstrike. The warlord protected his grave from plunderers with mechanisms created by the keepers. Volund did not want anyone to seize his beloved rifle even after his death.\n<BR/><BR/>\nFortunately, Titanstrike did not remain in the grave of that upstart warlord forever. This weapon was not made to spread terror and conquer. Its purpose is to defend Azeroth.\n<BR/><BR/>\nNow this masterpiece of Mimiron can fulfill its original mission. It will strike down any darkness that awakens, and its thunderous shot will bring death to all who threaten this world and its peoples.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5933', '<HTML><BODY>\n<p></p>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-hunter-thasdorah" align="center"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="125"/>\n<H3 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners</H3>\n<BR/>\n<P>\nIf only we could see Quel''Thalas again in all its splendor. Thas''dorah was made to help the defenders of the high elf kingdom. But the glorious history of this weapon has made it something more. From Azeroth it passed into the darkest corners of the Twisting Nether and destroyed warriors of the Burning Legion in the very domains of the demons.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe bow''s true wielder has yet to be found. But Alleria Windrunner was determined and practical; she insisted that a worthy defender once again strike fear into the Legion''s commanders with this weapon.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5934', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 1</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nNearly 7,000 years ago, the high elves founded their new home in the Eastern Kingdoms. Their journey there was not easy, and the land where they settled was hardly fit for habitation. But the result exceeded all expectations.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAt the intersection of magical ley lines, the elves built the city of Silvermoon. Nearby lay the Sunwell, a wellspring of pure arcane magic. The kingdom of Quel''Thalas became a dazzling monument to the great civilization that once dwelled on the shores of the Well of Eternity.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5935', 'The magic of the Sunwell spread through the forests of Eversong, and the oldest tree in the forest began to absorb it. This mighty tree was called Tas''ala, which means "Light of the Forest." The glow of eternal spring that radiated from its enchanted crown seemed to flood all the surrounding lands.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIt was from the branches of this very tree that this bow would be made. It would become the emblem of a high elf family whose members had been famed for their heroism for millennia.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5936', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 2</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe high elves of Quel''Thalas constantly had to fend off the Amani trolls. To hold back their onslaught, the elves raised several monolithic runestones that served as wards against invaders. These stones also helped the elves conceal their use of arcane magic from the Burning Legion.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe wards kept enemies from reaching Silvermoon, largely thanks to the source of their power: elven sorcerers had linked the runestones to Tas''ala. In this way the might of nature magic complemented the strength of arcane spells.\n<BR/><BR/>\nBeyond the city, however, the tree''s power could not protect the high elves. For that they needed skilled warriors.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5937', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 3</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe Amani trolls feared to attack Silvermoon directly. Magic held them back for several millennia, but in the end they went to war against the elves once more. This conflict became known as the Troll Wars and claimed many lives. To defend Quel''Thalas, a special order was established: the order of rangers.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe greatest of the first rangers was Talanas Windrunner, a master archer. Rising swiftly through the ranks thanks to his courage and valor, he became the first Ranger-General of Silvermoon. In honor of his appointment, the rulers of Silvermoon ordered a weapon crafted for him that befitted his new rank.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe high elven smiths set to work at once.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5938', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 4</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThree skilled craftsmen labored over Talanas''s glorious bow. After carving its stave from a branch of Tas''ala, they steeped its wood in water from the Sunwell.\n<BR/><BR/>\nFilled with the might of arcane magic, the bow began to change. It hardened, becoming nearly indestructible, yet it retained its bond with the nature magic embodied in the Tas''ala tree.\n<BR/><BR/>\nWhen the bow was lifted from the Sunwell, it radiated power. Talanas knew it was a priceless gift. He named his weapon Thas''dorah, which means "valor of the forest."\n<BR/><BR/>\nThen he vowed that Silvermoon would never fall so long as this bow defended it.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5939', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 5</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nTalanas kept his promise. The bow Thas''dorah was made so skillfully that even the most inexperienced archer could hit a target from a hundred paces. When Talanas loosed it, every arrow sang beautifully on its way to the mark.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe Amani trolls soon began to fear this bow: they understood that if they saw it, an arrow from it might strike them down. They were forced to retreat, and the high elves were able to found settlements deep in the forests.\n<BR/><BR/>\nTalanas Windrunner''s role in winning the lands of Quel''Thalas made him a legend for generations to come.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5940', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 6</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe bow Thas''dorah was passed down through the Windrunner line from generation to generation for long millennia. The elves of this line devoted their lives to the defense of Quel''Thalas. In every generation there were those who took this bow into the forests and defended the elves'' lands from invaders.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe bow Thas''dorah never left those lands, so it was always filled with the radiant magic of the Sunwell and the surrounding forests. Little by little the bow was tempered, acquiring new and wondrous properties.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIts magical power only grew. It began to manifest in unexpected ways. For instance, beside an arrow loosed at a foe, other, magical arrows might appear, flying toward the same target.\n<BR/><BR/>\nEven after the end of the Troll Wars, the bow Thas''dorah helped defend the elves'' lands from many invasions.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5941', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 7</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nA few years before the Horde''s invasion of Azeroth, the Ranger-General of Silvermoon was Lirisa Windrunner, who had three daughters: Alleria, Sylvanas, and Vereesa.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIt was expected that Alleria, the eldest daughter, would one day take her mother''s place, but she cared nothing for tradition and had no intention of living up to anyone''s expectations. She decided to find a more practical use for her talents and joined the Farstriders, high elf rangers who carried out operations all across Azeroth. She won several celebrated victories, and her name quickly became well known. One day the post of Ranger-General passed to her sister, Sylvanas.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThas''dorah, however, remained with Lirisa. Only upon her death did it pass to Alleria.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5942', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 8</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the Horde invaded Azeroth, at first it was not even taken seriously. Even after the orcs burned Stormwind to the ground, only a few rulers were concerned. The lords of Silvermoon had no intention of giving the humans of Lordaeron any significant aid, but Alleria decided that orcs possessed by demonic power posed an enormous threat to the whole world. Defying orders, she led her rangers to Lordaeron and fought valiantly on the side of the Alliance.\n<BR/><BR/>\nOnly when the orcs joined forces with the Amani trolls and turned their gaze toward Quel''Thalas did the high elves grasp the full scale of the catastrophe. Many elven rangers, including their leader, Lirisa Windrunner herself, fell in the bloody battle.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5943', 'Sylvanas Windrunner became the new Ranger-General, but she gave Thas''dorah to her elder sister Alleria. Sylvanas needed to defend Quel''Thalas, and she reasoned that Alleria, as one of the Farstriders, would be more effective on the front lines.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHer instinct did not fail her. Alleria became a veritable nightmare to the orcs and trolls; her arrows mowed down countless hordes of enemies.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5944', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 9</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen the Second War ended, the orcs returned to their homeworld of Draenor and rallied around a new chieftain there. Ner''zhul, the former chieftain of the Shadowmoon clan, declared that he would open portals to unexplored worlds, promising the orcs fabulous riches. The Alliance decided he had to be stopped. Alleria volunteered for the expeditionary force as a scout. She took Thas''dorah with her to Draenor.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe thirst for vengeance weighed heavily upon her. Only when she grew closer to a human paladin named Turalyon did she realize that this war was not yet the end, and she began to think about the future.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5945', 'The Alliance expedition attacked the Horde''s forces all across Draenor, but in the end Ner''zhul really did manage to open portals to other worlds. The surge of magical energy was so powerful that Draenor was quite literally torn apart.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe Alliance soldiers who failed to escape Draenor through the Dark Portal, including Alleria and Turalyon, were presumed dead.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5946', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 10</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWhen Thas''dorah was discovered on Niskara, one of the Legion''s worlds, hope arose that Alleria might have survived the catastrophe on Draenor after all. Vereesa was certain that her elder sister had continued her crusade against evil, standing against the Burning Legion itself, pursuing the demons into the most secret depths of the Twisting Nether.\n<BR/><BR/>\nJust as Thas''dorah had absorbed the energy of its homeworld, it also drew in the chaotic magic of the Void. Its arrows grew still more powerful and deadly, bringing certain doom to the servants of darkness.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe bow became a symbol of resistance to the Legion, a means of rallying Azeroth''s greatest hunters and leading them into battle against the hordes of demons.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5947', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Thas''dorah, Legacy of the Windrunners, Part 11</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nQuel''Thalas was no longer what it had been. The Tas''ala tree from which the bow was carved was cut down on the orders of Arthas Menethil. On its withered roots now stands the fortress of Deathholme. In the clashes with the Scourge, the high elves suffered enormous losses, and the few who survived later came to call themselves blood elves in memory of that tragedy.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThis bow was made to protect the innocent from the forces of evil. Over the years it has carried out its mission brilliantly, in this world and in many others.\n<BR/><BR/>\nNow it has returned to Azeroth once more. Loosed by one of this world''s greatest heroes, the arrows of Thas''dorah will soon strike at its enemies again.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5948', '<HTML><BODY>\n<p></p>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Pictures\\artifactbook-hunter-talonclaw" align="center"/>\n<IMG src="Interface\\Common\\spacer" align="center" width="1" height="155"/>\n<H3 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods</H3>\n<BR/>\n<P>\nThis spear, made before the War of the Ancients, is distinguished by its unique curved head shaped like an eagle''s beak. The energy of the Wild Gods, mighty animal Ancients, bound within it makes it incredibly powerful and one of a kind. Talonclaw has many times helped the tauren of Azeroth defend their homeland against a multitude of invaders. The spear was passed from generation to generation to the finest hunter of the Highmountain tribes until it was lost. The tauren will hold the wielder of their legendary weapon in great esteem, but the demons of the Legion will also hound them tirelessly.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5949', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 1</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nLong before the War of the Ancients, the tauren tribes wandered the forests of ancient Kalimdor. The Highmountain tribes were then led by Chieftain Moren Highmountain, who saved On''ara, a Wild God in the form of a huge eagle, from an attack by fire elementals. In gratitude for her rescue, the eagle placed a part of her essence into Moren''s spear, endowing it with powerful magical properties. Moren carved On''ara''s name on the spear''s shaft, and since then, in her honor, it has been called the Spear of the Great Eagle.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIn memory of the heroes whom fate brought him together with, the mighty foes he defeated, and the grand events he witnessed, Moren carved their names on the Spear of the Great Eagle as well. His successors who wielded the spear after him continued this tradition. In this way the spear gradually became the living history of the Highmountain tribe.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5950', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 2</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nEruna, Moren''s granddaughter, was to become the tribe''s new chieftain. But before taking on such a great responsibility, she took the Spear of the Great Eagle and set out on a pilgrimage that was to serve as a kind of rite of initiation. In the northern lands she encountered a group of strange tentacled creatures threatening a family of night elves. They were servants of the Old Gods, who had long slept in the hidden corners of the world and were only now beginning to awaken. Eruna attacked them, and suddenly a huge, mighty bear joined her; as it later turned out, this was an Ancient named Ursoc. Together, Eruna and Ursoc drove off the strange creatures.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5951', 'Having watched Eruna Highmountain in battle, Ursoc judged her worthy of his gift. The great bear told her that a great darkness was coming and the world would need many strong warriors and leaders like her. To aid in the coming struggle, he blessed her spear, making it even stronger. In gratitude, Eruna carved his name upon it, continuing the living history of the Spear of the Great Eagle, and returned to her tribe, ready to lead it.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5952', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 3</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nMany years after Eruna''s death, the spear passed to Huln Highmountain. Faced with the invasion of the Burning Legion, Halne united many tauren tribes under his banner and joined the night elves, who were already fighting the demons.\n<BR/><BR/>\nIn one fierce battle, Halne tried to save a two-headed Ancient wolf named Omen from the Legion, but the mighty beast was already mortally wounded and poisoned by fel venom. Resisting desperately, with his dying breath Omen placed a fragment of his still-uncorrupted power into the spear. Later, Halne carved the Ancient''s name on the spear so that his deed would live forever in the memory of descendants.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5953', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 4</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nDuring the War of the Ancients, Halne fought side by side with many outstanding warriors who would later write their names into history forever. Among them was the night elf Jarod Shadowsong, who saved Halne''s life in one of the last battles of that war. Halne carved the names of these warriors on the shaft of his spear as well. Once he met Malfurion Stormrage himself, the greatest of all druids.\n<BR/><BR/>\nThe elders of the Highmountain tribe tell the story of their meeting this way.\n"Halne handed the spear to the druid, who examined it carefully, noting the unusual rounded shape of the head, like an eagle''s beak. Then Malfurion read the names carved on the shaft. He spoke at length with each of the spirits bound within the spear and finally nodded in approval."\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5954', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 5</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe tauren elders maintain that the Doomlord Cyborn had set himself the goal of utterly annihilating the tauren at all costs, and Halne swore to kill him. The two bitter enemies met face to face in the battle for the Well of Eternity, and their duel ended when Halne, whom nothing seemed able to save, struck Cyborn a nearly fatal blow straight to the face. The demon had no time to turn the blow aside, and the spear, imbued with the power of the spirits, pierced his left eye, half blinding him.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5955', 'The battle was Halne''s, and he was about to finish the Doomlord, but he ran out of time: Cyborn and nearly all the demons in central Kalimdor were cast out of Azeroth. In the Twisting Nether the Doomlord licked his wounds and, awaiting the Burning Legion''s next campaign, swore to take revenge on Halne and all the tauren and to destroy the weapon that had blinded him in one eye. But Halne was not fated to find even a brief respite. Soon after the battle, the Well of Eternity exploded, and Azeroth shuddered with terrible earthquakes. Kalimdor began to break apart, and Halne and his people had to flee for their lives.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5956', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 6</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nAfter the War of the Ancients, the paths of the tauren tribes diverged. The ancient homeland of the tauren was destroyed in the Great Sundering, and Halne led his people in search of a new home. They visited many places in an Azeroth transformed by catastrophe before at last arriving on the Broken Isles, where they settled and founded the village of Thunder Totem at the foot of the island''s tallest mountain.\n<BR/><BR/>\nHaving found his people a new refuge, Halne bequeathed the Spear of the Great Eagle to his successor and went to join his forefathers. Legend has it that On''ara herself raised the great chieftain''s spirit to the heavens.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5957', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 7</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nMany years after Halne''s death, the twin sisters Arien and Gardrael came to lead the Highmountain tribes. Arien was the more experienced warrior and was finely attuned to nature, while everyone considered Gardrael the wiser and more prudent. So as not to divide their tribe between the two sisters, the aging chieftain named Gardrael her successor; Arien became Keeper of the Mountain, and the Spear of the Great Eagle passed to her.\n<BR/><BR/>\nBoth sisters were quite content with their lot. Gardrael was chieftain of the tribe, while Arien stood guard over its peace and welfare, aided considerably by the blessings of the Wild Gods bestowed upon her weapon. And in honor of those Wild Gods, friends of her ancestors, Arien gave her spear a new name: Talonclaw.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5958', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 8</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nExcerpts from the diary of Arien Highmountain, in which she describes her meeting with the night elves of the Unseen Path.\n<BR/><BR/>\n"Gardrael and I were leading the night elves up the mountain. When we were almost at the summit, Talonclaw began to glow. Obeying an inner voice, I raised it to the sky, and we all beheld a wondrous sight: golden light streamed from the spear''s head and took the shape of a huge eagle! It was then that my sister and I remembered the tales of our tribe and understood that On''ara herself had blessed this place. And at once it became clear as day to us that this mountain should be named Talon Peak."\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5959', 'It was only thanks to the support and help of the tauren that the hunters of the Unseen Path, who had come to the Broken Isles to guard them against the Legion, were able to settle there and make their new home, Trueshot Lodge. Together they raised three tall towers, known as the Three Talons. Day and night the order''s sentries kept a keen watch over everything that happened in the area, so as to be ready for the Burning Legion''s return, should it come to pass. Elves and tauren learned much from one another, and Arien herself in time joined the ranks of the Unseen Path, standing alongside its finest warriors.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5960', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 9</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe tradition of passing Talonclaw to the finest hunter of the Highmountain tribe was observed for many generations. Whenever the tribe was threatened or faced trials, the spear did not stand idle.\n<BR/><BR/>\nOnce, a rumor spread that some mighty beast had appeared in the forests of Stormheim. Nalm Highmountain took his spear and set off for those parts to track the beast and try to tame it, or kill it. The hunter was given a lavish send-off, with a sumptuous feast and the singing of the epic ballad "The Song of Talonclaw" for good luck. No one doubted that Nalm would succeed, and that peace and tranquility would soon return to their land.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5961', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 10</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nSeveral weeks passed, but there was no word from Nalm. A rescue party was sent after him. The tauren combed the entire area high and low, but there was no trace of either the famed hunter or his spear. And the beast, it seemed, was still alive: several of the rescuers went missing.\n<BR/><BR/>\nAfterward, many young hunters set out in search of the mysterious creature. Almost none of them returned, and nothing was heard of the spear from the moment Nalm vanished until On''ara led you to it. To find the legendary weapon of our ancestors is a great feat, and your name will now endure through the ages.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5962', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Talonclaw, Spear of the Wild Gods,<BR/>Part 11</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nWith the spear of the Wild Gods you will become a great hunter. In all of Azeroth only a handful will be your equal. It grants you exceptional strength, agility, and ferocity, and no beast or enemy will be able to escape you. Many hunters prefer bows or rifles, but you, like Huln Highmountain himself, do not fear to meet the enemy face to face in a deadly grapple. You have mastered every hunter''s skill on the ground, but now you have a chance to feel what it is like to strike down a foe like On''ara herself, the great eagle. The spear will let you survive in any conditions and become a true lord of the wilds. Like the eagle, you will need no one''s help, for you will have no equal.\n<BR/><BR/>'),
('5963', 'The spiritual energy bound within Talonclaw is so strong that the shrines and temples of the Wild Gods resonate with it; priests of the Wild Gods can also sense its presence. The wielder of this spear will be a welcome guest in any tauren settlement (unless, of course, they are at odds with the tauren). Remember, however, that Talonclaw may also attract the attention of the servants of Chaos, so it must be handled with care.\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('5964', '<HTML><BODY>\n<H2 align="center">Author''s Note</H2>\n<HR/>\n<P>\nThe research is proceeding at full speed. These texts contain an enormous amount of information. In time, however, I expect to decipher all of it.\n<BR/><BR/>\nCheck back when the next study is complete, and I will continue compiling this encyclopedia.\n</P>\n<BR/>\n<P align="right">\nHolt Thunderhorn of the Unseen Path\n</P>\n</BODY></HTML>'),
('6789', 'It appears these boots have been exposed to a flow of arcane energy of incredible strength.\n\nInside, each is marked "Heinrich."'),
('6883', 'An incredible discovery! I managed to summon a simulacrum of life in the shape of a small green dragon, but at first I almost decided it was a simple fluke, since none of my colleagues were able to reproduce the spell.\n\nBut accidents are never accidents. As I slipped my hand into one of my many pockets, I very nearly burned it: a green dragon scale, it turns out, had been lying around in there. That was when everything fell into place. Evidently the staff required some object on which to focus the arcane energy!\n\nI engraved runes on the staff that were meant to focus the energy and repeated the experiment. This time I managed to summon only a little sheep, the simplest manifestation of magic.\n\nBut with the help of the scale, I once again summoned the dragon. There must be other objects that can serve as a focusing object for the staff, and then I will be able to summon other simulacra as well.\n\nNaturally, further experiments will be required...\n\n- Willem West');
